## The Mediator Pattern in C# – A Practical, Step‑by‑Step Guide

> **TL;DR**  
> *Mediator* is a behavioral pattern that lets you reduce coupling between components by introducing a **central hub** (the Mediator) that handles all communication.  
> In C#, you usually create:
> 1. An `IMediator` interface (or abstract class) with a `Send` method.  
> 2. A `Colleague` base class that keeps a reference to an `IMediator`.  
> 3. Concrete Colleague classes that publish events to the mediator.  
> 4. A concrete Mediator that receives those events and orchestrates the interaction between colleagues.

Below is a complete, self‑contained example that you can paste into a console project and run. After the example, we’ll discuss variations, pitfalls, and a quick look at the popular **MediatR** library (which is essentially a production‑ready Mediator implementation).

---

## 1. The Problem

Imagine a UI with several components that need to talk to each other:

```text
[Button]  <->  [TextBox]  <->  [ListView]
```

If each component knows the others directly, you end up with a tangled web of dependencies. When you change one component, you may need to touch many others.

The Mediator pattern says: **“Let all communication go through a single point.”** That point is the mediator.

---

## 2. Basic Design

| Role | Responsibility | Typical C# representation |
|------|----------------|---------------------------|
| **Mediator** | Orchestrates communication. | `interface IMediator { void Send(string message, Colleague sender); }` |
| **Colleague** | Represents a participant that knows the mediator but not other colleagues. | `abstract class Colleague { protected IMediator Mediator; ... }` |
| **Concrete Colleague** | Implements specific behaviour and sends messages to mediator. | `class Button : Colleague { void Click() => Mediator.Send("Clicked", this); }` |
| **Concrete Mediator** | Knows all colleagues and decides what to do with each message. | `class FormMediator : IMediator { ... }` |

---

## 3. Full Code Example

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

// ---------------------------
// 1. The Mediator interface
// ---------------------------
public interface IMediator
{
    void Send(string message, Colleague sender);
}

// ---------------------------
// 2. Abstract colleague
// ---------------------------
public abstract class Colleague
{
    protected IMediator mediator;

    protected Colleague(IMediator mediator)
    {
        this.mediator = mediator;
    }
}

// ---------------------------
// 3. Concrete colleagues
// ---------------------------

public class Button : Colleague
{
    public string Id { get; }
    public Button(string id, IMediator mediator) : base(mediator) => Id = id;

    public void Click()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"{Id} (Button) clicked.");
        mediator.Send("ButtonClicked", this);
    }
}

public class TextBox : Colleague
{
    public string Id { get; }
    public string Text { get; set; }

    public TextBox(string id, IMediator mediator) : base(mediator) => Id = id;

    public void UpdateText(string text)
    {
        Text = text;
        Console.WriteLine($"{Id} (TextBox) updated text to \"{Text}\".");
    }

    public void OnTextChanged()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"{Id} (TextBox) text changed.");
        mediator.Send("TextChanged", this);
    }
}

public class ListView : Colleague
{
    public string Id { get; }
    public List<string> Items { get; } = new List<string>();

    public ListView(string id, IMediator mediator) : base(mediator) => Id = id;

    public void AddItem(string item)
    {
        Items.Add(item);
        Console.WriteLine($"{Id} (ListView) added item \"{item}\". Total items: {Items.Count}");
    }
}

// ---------------------------
// 4. Concrete mediator
// ---------------------------

public class FormMediator : IMediator
{
    private readonly Button _button;
    private readonly TextBox _textBox;
    private readonly ListView _listView;

    public FormMediator(Button button, TextBox textBox, ListView listView)
    {
        _button = button;
        _textBox = textBox;
        _listView = listView;
    }

    public void Send(string message, Colleague sender)
    {
        // Here is the core logic: "who" sent the message and "what" happened
        // determines what other colleagues should do.

        if (sender == _button && message == "ButtonClicked")
        {
            // For example: copy text to list view
            _listView.AddItem(_textBox.Text);
        }
        else if (sender == _textBox && message == "TextChanged")
        {
            // Maybe enable button only when text is non-empty
            // (In a real UI you'd enable/disable the button; here we just log)
            Console.WriteLine($"Mediator: Button is {(string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_textBox.Text) ? "disabled" : "enabled")}");
        }
    }
}

// ---------------------------
// 5. Client (Main)
// ---------------------------

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        // Create colleagues
        Button button = null;      // forward declaration because of circular dependency
        TextBox textBox = null;
        ListView listView = null;

        // Create mediator with placeholders
        FormMediator mediator = new FormMediator(
            button = new Button("Btn1", null),
            textBox = new TextBox("Txt1", null),
            listView = new ListView("Lst1", null));

        // Now that mediator exists, set mediator references on colleagues
        // (Alternatively, let mediator pass itself into colleague constructors)
        button = new Button("Btn1", mediator);
        textBox = new TextBox("Txt1", mediator);
        listView = new ListView("Lst1", mediator);

        // Rebuild mediator with correct references
        mediator = new FormMediator(button, textBox, listView);

        // Simulate interactions
        textBox.UpdateText("Hello");
        textBox.OnTextChanged();   // Mediator decides to enable button

        button.Click();            // Mediator copies text to ListView

        textBox.UpdateText("");
        textBox.OnTextChanged();   // Mediator disables button
    }
}
```

### What’s happening?

1. **Colleagues** (`Button`, `TextBox`, `ListView`) know **only** about the mediator (`IMediator`).  
2. They trigger events (`Click`, `OnTextChanged`) which call `mediator.Send(...)`.  
3. The **Mediator** (`FormMediator`) receives the event, knows the *origin* (`sender`) and the *message*, and decides what other colleagues should do.  
4. No colleague knows about any other colleague’s implementation. All interaction flows through the mediator, dramatically reducing coupling.

---

## 4. Variations & Enhancements

| Variation | Why use it? | How to implement |
|-----------|-------------|------------------|
| **Generic `Send<TMessage>(TMessage message, Colleague sender)`** | Type safety & avoid string keys | Use generics & `typeof(TMessage)` as a key in a `Dictionary` |
| **Event‑based mediator** | More decoupled; can register listeners | Mediator exposes events; colleagues subscribe instead of calling `Send` |
| **Asynchronous Mediator** | I/O bound operations, UI responsiveness | Make `SendAsync` returning `Task` |
| **Composite Mediator** | Hierarchical UI or micro‑services | A mediator that delegates to child mediators |

### Generic Mediator Example

```csharp
public interface IMediator
{
    void Send<TMessage>(TMessage message, Colleague sender);
}

public class GenericMediator : IMediator
{
    private readonly Dictionary<Type, List<Action<Colleague, object>>> _handlers
        = new Dictionary<Type, List<Action<Colleague, object>>>();

    public void Register<TMessage>(Action<Colleague, TMessage> handler)
    {
        var type = typeof(TMessage);
        if (!_handlers.ContainsKey(type))
            _handlers[type] = new List<Action<Colleague, object>>();

        _handlers[type].Add((sender, msg) => handler(sender, (TMessage)msg));
    }

    public void Send<TMessage>(TMessage message, Colleague sender)
    {
        var type = typeof(TMessage);
        if (!_handlers.TryGetValue(type, out var handlers))
            return;

        foreach (var h in handlers)
            h(sender, message);
    }
}
```

Now colleagues send strongly‑typed messages instead of string identifiers.

---

## 5. Using MediatR (Production‑Ready Mediator)

The open‑source library **MediatR** (available on NuGet) implements a fully‑featured mediator with:

- Request/Response pattern (`IRequest<TResponse>`) for queries/commands.
- Notification pattern (`INotification`) for events.
- Pipeline behaviours (validation, logging, transactions).
- Async support out of the box.

```bash
dotnet add package MediatR
dotnet add package MediatR.Extensions.Microsoft.DependencyInjection
```

### Quick Sample

```csharp
using MediatR;
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;

public class CreateOrderCommand : IRequest<Guid>
{
    public string ProductName { get; }
    public int Quantity { get; }

    public CreateOrderCommand(string productName, int quantity)
    {
        ProductName = productName;
        Quantity = quantity;
    }
}

public class CreateOrderHandler : IRequestHandler<CreateOrderCommand, Guid>
{
    public Task<Guid> Handle(CreateOrderCommand request, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        // Persist order to DB...
        var orderId = Guid.NewGuid();
        Console.WriteLine($"Order {orderId} created for {request.Quantity}x {request.ProductName}");
        return Task.FromResult(orderId);
    }
}

public class Program
{
    static async Task Main()
    {
        var services = new ServiceCollection();
        services.AddMediatR(typeof(Program)); // registers handlers
        var provider = services.BuildServiceProvider();
        var mediator = provider.GetRequiredService<IMediator>();

        var orderId = await mediator.Send(new CreateOrderCommand("Gadget", 5));
        Console.WriteLine($"Created order {orderId}");
    }
}
```

MediatR abstracts away the manual mediator wiring. You only declare request/response types and handlers; the library wires them together automatically. This is ideal for large applications or micro‑services.

---

## 6. When NOT to Use a Mediator

| Situation | Reason |
|-----------|--------|
| **Very small projects** | The added indirection may be overkill. |
| **Components that need to be reused in different contexts** | Mediator couples components to a specific mediator. |
| **Performance‑critical path** | Extra method calls may add latency (though usually negligible). |
| **When a publish/subscribe (pub‑sub) pattern is more suitable** | If you need many-to-many communication, a mediator is still okay, but a message bus or event aggregator may be clearer. |

---

## 7. Take‑away Checklist

1. **Define** the `IMediator` contract (`Send`/`SendAsync`).  
2. **Create** a `Colleague` base that keeps a mediator reference.  
3. **Implement** concrete colleagues that call `mediator.Send`.  
4. **Build** a concrete mediator that knows all colleagues and orchestrates.  
5. **Instantiate** everything in a client (or a DI container).  
6. **Test** by firing events and watching how the mediator coordinates actions.  
7. **Consider** a library like MediatR if you need a robust, battle‑tested solution.

With this pattern, your components stay loosely coupled, easier to test, and each piece of functionality can evolve independently. Happy coding!
